{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"operator-overloading","title":"Operator Overloading","group":"Classes & Objects","order":29,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">You can give operators like <code>+</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>&lt;&lt;</code> and <code>[]</code> a meaning for your own types, so they read like built-ins. Overload only when the meaning is obvious.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>A value type with arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nstruct Vec2 {\n    double x = 0, y = 0;\n\n    Vec2&amp; operator+=(const Vec2&amp; o) { x += o.x; y += o.y; return *this; }   // member: modifies *this\n    Vec2 operator-() const { return {-x, -y}; }                             // unary minus\n    bool operator==(const Vec2&amp;) const = default;                           // C++20: memberwise ==\n};\n\nVec2 operator+(Vec2 a, const Vec2&amp; b) { return a += b; }        // non-member, built from +=\nVec2 operator*(const Vec2&amp; v, double k) { return {v.x * k, v.y * k}; }\nVec2 operator*(double k, const Vec2&amp; v) { return v * k; }         // allow 2 * v too\n\nstd::ostream&amp; operator&lt;&lt;(std::ostream&amp; os, const Vec2&amp; v) {       // printing\n    return os &lt;&lt; '(' &lt;&lt; v.x &lt;&lt; \", \" &lt;&lt; v.y &lt;&lt; ')';\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Vec2 a{1, 2}, b{3, 4};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a + b &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; -a &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; 2 * b &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    a += b;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (a == Vec2{4, 6}) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (a != b) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>(4, 6) (-1, -2) (6, 8)\n(4, 6) 1 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>Member or non-member?</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Usually</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>=</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>()</code>, <code>-&gt;</code>, conversion</td><td><strong>Must</strong> be members</td><td>Language rule</td></tr><tr><td><code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, <code>++</code>, <code>--</code>, unary ops</td><td>Member</td><td>They modify / belong to <code>*this</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>==</code>, <code>&lt;</code></td><td>Non-member (often <code>friend</code>)</td><td>Symmetric: allows conversions on <em>both</em> sides (<code>2 * v</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code> (streams)</td><td>Non-member</td><td>Left operand is the stream, not your type</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Comparison with <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> (C++20)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;compare&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Version {\n    int major, minor, patch;\n    auto operator&lt;=&gt;(const Version&amp;) const = default;   // generates &lt;, &lt;=, &gt;, &gt;=, == and !=\n};\n\nstruct Employee {\n    std::string name;\n    int salary;\n    std::strong_ordering operator&lt;=&gt;(const Employee&amp; o) const { return salary &lt;=&gt; o.salary; }\n    bool operator==(const Employee&amp; o) const { return salary == o.salary; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (Version{1, 2, 3} &lt; Version{1, 10, 0}) &lt;&lt; (Version{2, 0, 0} &gt;= Version{1, 9, 9}) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::vector&lt;Employee&gt; team{{\"Bo\", 50}, {\"Al\", 90}, {\"Cy\", 70}};\n    std::sort(team.begin(), team.end());\n    for (const auto&amp; e : team) std::cout &lt;&lt; e.name &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>11\nBo Cy Al</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Subscript, call, increment</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n\nclass IntArray {\n    int data_[4] = {};\npublic:\n    int&amp; operator[](std::size_t i) { return data_[i]; }              // non-const: allows writes\n    const int&amp; operator[](std::size_t i) const { return data_[i]; }  // const overload for const objects\n};\n\nstruct Counter {\n    int n = 0;\n    Counter&amp; operator++() { ++n; return *this; }                 // prefix\n    Counter operator++(int) { Counter old = *this; ++n; return old; }   // postfix (dummy int)\n    int operator()(int step) { return n += step; }               // call operator: a functor\n    explicit operator bool() const { return n != 0; }            // conversion\n};\n\nint main() {\n    IntArray a;\n    a[2] = 7;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a[2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    Counter c;\n    Counter before = c++;\n    ++c;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; before.n &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c.n &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; c(10) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; static_cast&lt;bool&gt;(c) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    if (c) std::cout &lt;&lt; \"non-zero\\n\";      // explicit bool still works in conditions\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7\n0 2 12 1\nnon-zero</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Copy assignment</h3>\n<p><code>operator=</code> should return <code>*this</code> by reference and survive self-assignment. The <em>copy-and-swap</em> idiom gets both, plus exception safety — see <strong>Copy Semantics</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Can't overload</th><th>Shouldn't overload</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>::</code> <code>.</code> <code>.*</code> <code>?:</code> <code>sizeof</code> <code>typeid</code></td><td><code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code> (lose short-circuiting), <code>,</code>, unary <code>&amp;</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Keep the natural meaning: <code>+</code> shouldn't modify its operands, <code>==</code> must be symmetric and consistent with <code>!=</code>, and <code>&lt;</code> must be a strict weak ordering if you want to use the type in <code>std::sort</code> or <code>std::map</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why return a reference from operator+= but a value from operator+?</summary><div><p><code>a += b</code> modifies <code>a</code> and yields <code>a</code> itself, so returning <code>*this</code> by reference allows chaining without copies. <code>a + b</code> produces a <em>new</em> value, leaving both operands untouched — returning a reference would dangle.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is operator&lt;&lt; for streams a non-member function?</summary><div><p>The left operand of <code>std::cout &lt;&lt; obj</code> is the stream. A member <code>operator&lt;&lt;</code> would have to be a member of <code>std::ostream</code>, which you can't modify. As a non-member it takes <code>(std::ostream&amp;, const T&amp;)</code> and returns the stream for chaining. Make it a <code>friend</code> if it needs private members.</p></div></details></section>"}